    "annotations": [
      {
        "kind": "racemisation_ratio",
        "value": "Not applicable at the carbon the product forms at. After the 1,2 hydride shift the cation sits at C3, which carries two methyl groups and is not a stereocentre, so bromide returning to either face gives the same compound.",
        "justification": "Authored as an explicit not applicable rather than left off, for the reason given in good-sn1-solvolysis-of-tert-butyl-bromide-in-water: nothing in this package can see chirality, so the claim has to come from a person. Worth stating twice over on this fixture because the carbon that LOST the leaving group, C2, was a stereocentre in the substrate and the carbon that gains the nucleophile is not the same carbon. The rearrangement is exactly why the stereochemical question moves, and a reader who assumed the product centre inherits the substrate's would get this wrong."
